h tool guesses. Pandas leaves it naive. JavaScript's `Date` and many databases read it as local time. Depending on the viewer's timezone, it can land hours away from rows 1 and 3, even though it was probably meant to be about 10:05 UTC.\n- **Mixed columns:** Pandas can fail to parse a column with both aware and naive values, or fall back to strings or `object`. Either way, the sorting and bucketing come out wrong.\n\n**Fix:**\n1. Find out what timezone the source system used for the naive rows. I'm guessing UTC from the neighbouring rows, but only the source can confirm it.\n2. Normalize everything to UTC on load, for example `pd.to_datetime(df.ts, utc=True)`, after attaching the correct zone to the naive rows.\n3. Better still, fix the exporter so it always writes an explicit offset.\n\nI only checked this file. I haven't looked at the chart code, so I can't say which parser is shifting the values.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}